---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { publishable, because } from "../lib/site.ts";
import { SITE } from "../site.ts";
import { EVIDENCE_LABEL, ATTRIBUTION_LABEL, WINDOW_LABEL, GRADE_DESCRIPTION } from "../lib/format.ts";
import { EVIDENCE, ATTRIBUTION, WINDOW } from "../schema.ts";

// One live example per grade value: the highest-ranked publishable entry that carries it.
const sorted = [...publishable].sort((a, b) => (a.section === b.section ? a.rank - b.rank : a.section === "because" ? -1 : 1));
const example = (key: "evidence" | "attribution" | "window", value: string) => sorted.find((e) => e.grades[key] === value);
const gb = because.find((e) => e.id === "golden-bridge-ii-stroke");
---
<Base title="Method" description="The four tests every entry passes, the three grades, and what is and is not automated.">
  <article class="prose" data-pagefind-body>
    <h1 class="page-title">Method</h1>
    <p class="thesis">{SITE.thesis}</p>
    <p>Readers of this site have been burned by AI hype. So the registry earns credibility by what it leaves out, by publishing what did not work, and by dating every benefit by when it happened rather than when the paper came out.</p>

    <h2 id="four-tests">The four tests</h2>
    <p>An entry starts "Because of AI, …" only if all four hold:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>It reached people.</strong> A benefit arrived for actual humans, not a benchmark score or a demo.</li>
      <li><strong>It was measured against something.</strong> A control arm, a prior period, a human benchmark, another model. The comparison is named on every entry.</li>
      <li><strong>The AI did something that would not otherwise have happened.</strong> The attribution grade says how cleanly we can tell.</li>
      <li><strong>We know when the benefit occurred.</strong> Not when the result was published.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>A result that passes the first three but produced no benefit goes to <a href="/not-because-of-ai">Not because of AI</a>, under the same grades. A candidate that has not yet passed them goes to <a href="/not-yet">Not yet</a>, with a note on what would change that.</p>

    <h2 id="grades">Three grades, never one score</h2>
    <p>Every entry carries three separate stamps. They are a vocabulary, not a formula. They are never added up, and no entry has a single score. The same sentences below appear when you hover or focus a stamp anywhere on the site.</p>

    <h3 id="evidence">Evidence</h3>
    <ul>
      {EVIDENCE.map((v) => { const ex = example("evidence", v); return (
        <li id={`evidence-${v}`}><strong>{EVIDENCE_LABEL[v]}.</strong> {GRADE_DESCRIPTION.evidence[v]} {ex && <span class="example">Example: <a href={`/entry/${ex.id}`}>{ex.headline}</a></span>}</li>
      ); })}
    </ul>

    <h3 id="attribution">Attribution</h3>
    <ul>
      {ATTRIBUTION.map((v) => { const ex = example("attribution", v); return (
        <li id={`attribution-${v}`}><strong>{ATTRIBUTION_LABEL[v]}.</strong> {GRADE_DESCRIPTION.attribution[v]} {ex && <span class="example">Example: <a href={`/entry/${ex.id}`}>{ex.headline}</a></span>}</li>
      ); })}
    </ul>
    <p>The cleanest counterfactual is where no human alternative existed: tuberculosis X-rays with no radiologist to read them, sperm no technician could find. Bundled and AI-versus-AI results are real results that say less about AI than they appear to. We label them rather than hide them.</p>

    <h3 id="window">Window</h3>
    <ul>
      {WINDOW.map((v) => { const ex = example("window", v); return (
        <li id={`window-${v}`}><strong>{WINDOW_LABEL[v]}.</strong> {GRADE_DESCRIPTION.window[v]} {ex && <span class="example">Example: <a href={`/entry/${ex.id}`}>{ex.headline}</a></span>}</li>
      ); })}
    </ul>
    <p>The window is the twelve months ending on the registry's reference date, currently {SITE.asOf}. Window grades are editorial and change only through a reviewed edit, so an entry can sit at the edge of the window for a short while before it is re-graded. Pre-window entries stay on the main list with a hatched stamp: the result is real, it is just not new.</p>

    <h2 id="achieved-vs-published">Achieved is not published</h2>
    <p>This is the registry's most common mistake, and the one we work hardest to avoid. Every row carries a lag bar: the span the benefit was achieved as a solid bar, the publication as an orange tick, and the gap between them hatched and labelled when it is six months or more. The dates themselves are on the entry page.</p>
    {gb && (
      <p>The worked example is <a href={`/entry/${gb.id}`}>GOLDEN BRIDGE II</a>, a cluster-randomized trial across 77 Chinese hospitals published in the BMJ in March 2026. The trial is superb. Its patients were enrolled between January 2021 and June 2023. So the benefit happened years before the paper, and the entry is graded <a href="#window-pre">pre-window</a>, not in-window. We check methods sections and trial registries before trusting a headline date.</p>
    )}

    <h2 id="pipeline">How entries get here: no human in the loop, and what replaces one</h2>
    <p>No human reviews entries. The editor writes the rules on this page (the four tests, the three grades, where to look, what counts as a primary source) and does not read entries before they publish. The rules are public and fixed by the editor; the pipeline applies them; readers check the work.</p>
    <p>Every morning a scout scans trial registries, outcome journals, forecast verification reports, and program evaluations for leads. A drafting model from one vendor reads the primary source in full and writes the entry, including a <strong>receipt</strong> for every number: the verbatim sentence in the source that supports it, with where to find it. Every number on an entry links to the sentence in the primary source that supports it. Then a verifying model from a <em>different</em> vendor receives only the source text and the entry, never the drafter's reasoning, and independently checks each receipt, grades the entry, and applies the four tests. Code decides the rest: <strong>two models from different vendors must independently agree</strong> on every receipt and every grade, or the entry is held. Any receipt the source does not support, any grade the two disagree on, any failed test, a conflict the source shows that the entry does not state, a source that could not be read in full, or a vendor that could not be reached: held. Nothing publishes on one vendor's say-so. The registry does not vote on facts and does not call a tiebreaker.</p>
    <p>Entries that were live before the gate existed (October 2026) stay published until a gate run holds them, and each shows its verification once it has one; every entry added or changed since goes through the gate before it publishes. Held entries and their disagreements are published, not hidden: the <a href="/changelog#held">held list</a> names each one with the reason and both verdicts, and the full output of every verification is committed to the <a href={`${SITE.repo}/tree/main/data/verifications`}>public repository</a>, with the model that drafted and the model that verified named on every entry page. A reader who disagrees opens a <a href={`${SITE.repo}/issues/new/choose`}>dispute</a>; both vendors assess it against the primary source, and the entry is amended, the dispute closed with the reasoning, or the entry held while they disagree. No human step there either.</p>
    <p id="conflict">The conflict of interest is real and stays: the models grading a registry of AI benefits have an interest in the answer. Cross-vendor checking plus receipts is how the registry handles that. It is not a claim to have removed it.</p>
    <p id="why-we-dont-auto-score">The cautionary case is an automated index that collected thousands of entries in a couple of months by having a language model read and score papers. Unrelated studies in it carry identical sub-scores, line for line, because the scorer produces the same shape of answer whatever it is shown. An index like that looks dense and reads as evidence. It is neither. The difference here is not that a model reads the paper. It is that a second model from another vendor must reach the same conclusion from the same text, that every number carries the sentence it came from, and that disagreement is published rather than averaged.</p>
    <p>Our rules, for the pipeline and for the editor's rulebook alike: a primary source for anything we put a number on, an absolute effect next to every relative one, company dashboards admitted only with the conflict stated and the comparison understood, press releases treated as leads rather than evidence, and no entry without a date the benefit actually occurred.</p>

    <h2 id="submit-dispute">Submit or dispute an entry</h2>
    <p>Both go through the public repository. <a href={`${SITE.repo}/issues/new/choose`}>Open an issue</a> to propose a new entry (bring the primary source and the achieved date) or to dispute an existing one (name the entry and the claim you disagree with). Every change to an entry is recorded in its history and in the <a href="/changelog">changelog</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
